body:
This is an interesting idea, and seems like it will be useful.

One issue with this approach for TypeScript users is that it creates another bag of possibly endless nullable properties that all will have type definitions on a single object up front, even if a mixin is not being used.

I.e. the set of known properties cannot be defined only as applied.

For example, if I want to use a single mixin, my IDE intellisense (for example an auto-complete popup) will show me all possible properties from all mixins, even those that I have not applied. And on top of that, although I have applied a certain mixin, TS will still tell me that the properties are nullable and force me to guard against null/undefined values.

## alternative 1

The class-factory mixin pattern that @justinfagnani mentioned avoids this problem at the class level: a class with mixins applied to it will only ever have type definitions for properties that belong to the applied mixins, and those properties will not be nullable (unless a property is designed to be nullable intentionally).

At this point, the HTMLElement class is already a huge bag of properties, so using class-factory mixins on a class that extends it won't necessarily be incredibly helpful because an auto-complete popup will still show so many properties that discoverability is still a little bit hampered.

But nonetheless, mixins on a class definition would still be helpful: if we type `.someProp` with our keyboard, which is intending to use a property from a certain mixin, then if the auto-complete popup does *not* show me the item, it *surely* means we do not have the mixin applied so we do not need to use this property in this class. However with the current proposal we will always see *all possible properties from all possible mixins* and we won't easily tell via auto-completion whether or not a property is actually defined to be used (we may even have to wait until runtime to see a runtime error).

Basically the existence or not of properties being statically known at compile/type check time is great.

LMK if you'd like some TypeScript playground samples to show what I mean.

## alternative 2

Another possibility is to use composition, by having composed features apply native behaviors to elements, and known properties would have type definitions on known classes, also never nullable (unless explicitly designed to be nullable):

```js
class MyEl extends HTMLElement {
  #buttonBehavior = HTMLBehaviors.SubmitButton(this)

  connectedCallback() {
    this.#buttonBehavior.knownProperty = {n:123}
  }
}
```

Where `HTMLBehaviors` is a namespace to avoid growing the global property bag too much to keep the IDE experience tidy, `SubmitButton` is a behavior class showing that behavior classes accept an element as the first arg, and `knownProperty` is auto-completable off of the `#buttonBehavior` prop with a known type (in this example) of `{n:number}` and not `{n:number} | undefined` because the type system *knows* the property exists.

## alternative 3

One more idea is, if behaviors should not be arbitrarily applied to any element from public code (and why not?), then the classes can be exposed via ElementInternals or a new similar pattern, and instantiated without needing to pass `(this)`, still providing per-behavior known properties:

```js
class MyEl extends HTMLElement {
  #internals = this.attachInternals()

  #behaviors = this.getBehaviors() // can be called only once, like attachInternals()
  // or
  // #behaviors = this.#internals.getBehaviors()

  #buttonBehavior = new this.#behaviors.SubmitButton()
// the element for which the behavior classes apply is implicit, internally in scope of the returned behavior class constructor.

  connectedCallback() {
    this.#buttonBehavior.knownProperty
  }
}
```

---

With all three alternative approaches, the IDE experience will be more useful, keeping away unnecessary null checking or non-null assertions.

@trusktr 

> One issue with this approach for TypeScript users is that it creates another bag of possibly endless nullable properties that all will have type definitions on a single object up front, even if a mixin is not being used.

Can you say more what you mean here? Automatically exposing properties is listed in open questions, but there's a lot of input here already against that idea. It's not part of the core proposal. Properties are instead available on the behavior objects themselves.

Thank you for the feedback! I've updated the explainer and the original description of this issue.

---

@LeaVerou, I agree that focusability and pseudo-class access are pressing needs that would benefit many use cases beyond submit buttons. However, I don't think we need to choose between primitive behaviors and bundled behaviors. Both can advance in parallel. The bundled approach (like `HTMLSubmitButtonBehavior`) addresses a concrete use case that frameworks are actively working around today (as documented in the [user research section](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#user-research) with Shoelace, Material Web, etc.). Starting with a focused behavior lets us validate the overall pattern (conflict resolution strategies, lifecycle semantics, etc.) before expanding to more capabilities.

---

@keithamus, I'm glad this new proposal resonates with you. I wanted to follow up on your points since several have been addressed in the newest update to the [explainer](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md):

***Userland*/developer-defined behaviors:** The section [developer-defined behaviors](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#developer-defined-behaviors) now explores how developers could create their own behaviors with `onAttached`/`onDetached` hooks. It also shows how this enables polyfilling new platform behaviors.

**Duplicate behaviors:** The explainer now specifies that duplicate behaviors throw a `TypeError`. This applies both to the initial `attachInternals()` call and to later modifications to `behaviorList`. See the [Duplicate behaviors](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#duplicate-behaviors) section.

**Naming consistency:** A new section for the API shape was added to discuss the tradeoffs of `behaviors` vs `behaviorList` and presents alternatives, see [naming consistency](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#naming-consistency). Feedback on the alternatives is greatly appreciated.

Additionally, there's also a new section discussing [behavior composition and conflict resolution](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#behavior-composition-and-conflict-resolution) (with [examples](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#framework-use-cases)). I believe @mfreed7 also suggested this.

Let me know if I missed anything, happy to incorporate more feedback.

---

@trusktr, thank you for raising the TypeScript ergonomics concern. I want to clarify one aspect that may help: in the current proposal, behavior state is accessed via the behavior object itself, not via properties automatically added to the element. For example:

```javascript
this._internals.behaviors.htmlSubmitButton.formAction = '/custom';
```

The element's public API doesn't change and web authors explicitly choose which properties to expose via getters/setters. This means your custom element class only has the properties you define. Would storing the behavior instance directly help? The explainer's [alternative 1 in naming consistency](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#alternative-1-use-behaviors-everywhere-instantiated-behaviors) shows a pattern where you instantiate behaviors and store them as properties:

   ```typescript
    #submitBehavior = new HTMLSubmitButtonBehavior();
    #internals = this.attachInternals({ behaviors: [this.#submitBehavior] });

    // Access behavior state directly via the stored reference.
    this.#submitBehavior.formAction = '/custom';
   ```

   With this pattern, TypeScript knows `#submitBehavior` is non-nullable because you instantiated it directly. Would this address your concern?

Regarding class-factory mixins: they're discussed in [Alternative 1 of the Alternatives Considered section](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#alternative-1-static-class-mixins). The main drawback is that behavior is fixed at class definition time. This prevents patterns like `<custom-button type="submit">` vs `<custom-button type="reset">` where a single element class handles multiple behaviors based on runtime state.

I think that `<input type=...>` is recognized as somewhat of an anti pattern that the ship already sailed for. Elements with statically known APIs would be preferable over `input type` which is like a bunch of elements all mashed up into one.

As for the workaround for types, indeed that would do the trick allowing me to place the behavior into a known non-nullable property. I didn't realize behaviors could be anonymous, so in that case it works out for static typing.

As for named behaviors, let's imagine for example that in the future an app has 100 or more behaviors (whether custom or built-in). Does this mean all the named behaviors would need to be registered onto an interface (similar to TypeScript's `HTMLElementTagNameNap` or`JSX.IntrinsicElements`) and thus `internals.behaviors` would have 100 or more nullable keys?

How do we keep named behaviors, but have a statically known set per element definition?

The main concern is that if developer-provided behaviors become possible, that could open up the door to thousands of behaviors in very large apps, and it would be nice not to have a list of thousands of *possible* behaviors for every element. In fact, some behaviors may be designed to work with specific types of elements such as only inputs or buttons, so seeing these behaviors also listed for any other element could be confusing.

Example:

```js
// this is a large app with 1000 behaviors for sake of example

class MyFormElement extends CoolFormElement {
  connectedCallback() {
    this.#internals = this.attachInternals({
      behaviors: [CoolFormBehavior]
    });
  }
}

// this element has nothing to do with CoolFormElement features.
class MyOtherElement extends TotallySomethingElse {
  connectedCallback() {
    this.#internals = this.attachInternals();

    this.#internals.behaviors.|
  }
}
```

In this example, the cursor is at `|`, and the IDE shows a popup with 1000 possible behaviors, including those that are strictly only useful for elements that extend from `CoolFormElement`.

Seeing the IDE tell us that CoolFormBehavior is a possible behavior, although it actually isn't, would not be the most ideal setup.

How can this issue be solved so that only relevant behaviors (those that are picked to be applied) are shown?

I generally like the approach of behaviors for web components. 👍 
However, I also share some of the concerns others have already brought up.

1. From my point of view, there is no clear benefit to gate most of the pseudo states behind behaviors. These could be configurable via ElementInternals:
    ```ts
      interface ElementInternals extends ARIAMixin {
        // Controls :enabled (false) and :disabled (true)
        disabled: boolean;
        // Equivalent to tabindex="-1" if set to true
        focusable: boolean;
        // Equivalent to tabindex="0" if set to true
        tabable: boolean;
        // Controls :checked (true)
        checked: boolean;
        // Controls :indeterminate (true)
        indeterminate: boolean;
        // Controls :in-range (true), :out-of-range (true) and neither (null)
        inRange: boolean | null;
        // Controls :required (true), :optional (false) and neither (null)
        required: boolean | null;
        // Indirectly controls :user-valid and :user-invalid
        userInteracted: boolean;
        // etc.
        ...
      }
    ```
2. The API shape seems a bit odd to me. I'm aware that mileage may vary, but I would prefer something like the class instance behaviors mentioned as alternative.

    Abbreviated example for button and text input
    ```ts
      interface ElementInternals extends ARIAMixin {
        // I am a novice when it comes to the IDL, so whether a Set works is unclear to me.
        // The convenience of just adding and deleting entries is my motivation for a Set.
        behaviors: Set<unknown>;
      }

      class MyButton extends HTMLElement {
        #internals: ElementInternals;
        #buttonBehavior: HTMLBehaviors.Button;

        set type(value: string) {
          // If only a singular type should be supported, then don't expose this setter
          // and the type could be hardcoded in the constructor. But that is up to the
          // developer.
          this.#buttonBehavior.type = value;
        }
        get type(): string {
          return this.#buttonBehavior.type;
        }

        set command(value: string) {
          this.#buttonBehavior.command = value;
        }
        get command(): string {
          return this.#buttonBehavior.command
        }

        // etc.

        constructor() {
          super();
          this.#internals = this.attachInternals();
          this.#buttonBehavior = new HTMLBehavior.Button(this, this.#internals);
          this.#internals.behaviors.add(this.#buttonBehavior)
        }
      }

      class MyTextInput extends HTMLElement {
        #internals: ElementInternals;
        #inputBehavior: HTMLBehaviors.TextInput;

        // expose input properties

        constructor() {
          super();
          this.#internals = this.attachInternals();
          this.#inputBehavior = new HTMLBehavior.TextInput(this, this.#internals);
          this.#internals.behaviors.add(this.#inputBehavior)
        }

        // expose input methods
      }
    ```
    This would cleanly constrain the API (properties or methods) to the corresponding behavior class and avoid property sprawl on the ElementInternals.

    I also have slight concerns about splitting the functionality of the button behaviors into separate behavior classes/constants, as this might necessitate transferring state between button behavior instances, when the type changes (however this might only be a problem with my suggested approach).
3. I do like the possibility for custom behaviors. I wonder whether the `onAttached` should receive the internals as that could also be passed to the constructor of the behavior. Or do you foresee a scenario where a behavior might be migrated between elements where this parameter would be useful? (Also naming for `onAttached`, etc. Should this be aligned with `...Callback()` terminology or are there other precedents for this?)

@noamr, thanks for testing that. I'd like to hear your thoughts on [Alternative API design 3](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/PlatformProvidedBehaviors/explainer.md#alternative-api-design-3-behavior-scoped-behaviordisabled) for the genuinely runtime-mutable case (`<x-input>` with ~14 types, or any other open-set behavior). In summary, attach the closed set of relevant behaviors at `attachInternals()` and toggle each one's contribution in response to attribute changes.

Limits note: as I mentioned in my previous reply, the on/off semantics there are still open, the mid-interaction edge cases are unresolved, and for elements with many possible types the all-attached cost grows linearly, which is an overhead concern (*God-like* element, as I've heard some devs describe this pattern).

